diff --git a/docs/limits.md b/docs/limits.md
index 5b4daee..68ffa34 100644
--- a/docs/limits.md
+++ b/docs/limits.md
@@ -110,6 +110,8 @@ Rules of the desk's records (the database refuses these, for a database created
 * Open files: at start-up the desk checks that the open-file limit of this computer covers `MAX_HANDLER_THREADS` (4 files per thread plus 96 for everything else; contract.md lists what the 4 and the 96 are made of). With the default 1024 threads that is 4192 open files. A computer whose hard limit is lower than that makes the desk exit with code 3; 4096 is a common default inside containers, so there raise the open-file limit of the operating system (for example `ulimit -n 8192`, or the container's own open-file setting).
   * The count is arithmetic (contract.md lists what the 4 and the 96 are made of; `test_connection_holds_counted_files` and `test_reserve_covers_its_items` check the database connection and the portal's connections); DNS lookups and the temporary files SQLite makes are not itemised in the 96. It raises the soft limit to exactly what is needed when the hard limit is unlimited or at least that, and otherwise refuses to start (exit code 3). The message names the open-file limit in force and what the desk needs.
 * Exit codes (read from `main` in `src/lead_desk/server.py`): 1 the demo servers cannot be reached at start-up, or a Python traceback (a constant that is out of range); 2 another desk holds the lock file, or the lock file cannot be taken for another reason (a folder, no permission); 3 the open-file limit is too low; 4 the desk refuses a setting (a number that is not plain digits or is outside its range, any other bad `DESK_*` value, a bind address that is not on this computer while a real service is set, a `db_path` of `:memory:`, a bad approvers file or steps file, a steps file that does not fit the steps already stored with the leads, or an AI adapter whose `timeout_seconds` is not a number above 0 or is above the AI call budget of 9 s; the message names the setting; an error that is not a refused setting never gets code 4); 5 the port could not be bound within `BIND_RETRY_SECONDS` (see above).
+* Exit codes of the helper scripts (`scripts/`, `load_tools/`) are separate from the desk's: 2 = bad usage or a refused input, 3 = the desk or a service cannot be reached, or a check of a run did not hold, 4 = `load_tools/check_invariants.py` refuses a log with more than `LOAD_TOTAL_MAX` rows. Some numbers are shared on purpose, each by two meanings that never occur in the same program: 3 is both "cannot be reached" and "a check did not hold", and 1 is both "stopped" and "adapters cannot be reached". `tests/test_r6_refusals.py::test_shared_exit_code_pairs_are_pinned` pins each pair, so a change of one number is noticed. The message line says which meaning applies.
+* A key or password (a file line or an environment value) must be at least 8 characters of plain printable ASCII with no space, tab or control character. A key with a non-ASCII character is refused in the file reader and for the environment value, before any money is reserved and before any connection is made. A TLS setting that weakens the check (no host-name check, no certificate check) is refused when the connection is made, not only when the object is built, and a CA file together with a ready-made TLS context is refused.
 * Stopping: Ctrl+C or SIGTERM stop the background job first and then give the lock file back. A second signal while that runs is ignored on purpose, so it cannot cut a step short. If the desk is still there after about 10 seconds, stop it by force (`kill -9` on Linux; on Windows, end the process); the lock is dropped and the next start puts every unfinished follow-up back.
 * Two limits apply. (1) At most `MAX_CONNECTIONS` (256) connections can be in their reading phase (request line, headers, body) at one time; a connection stops counting when the desk starts writing its reply, so a thread that is still finishing a follow-up does not count here. (2) At most `MAX_HANDLER_THREADS` (1024) handler threads exist in total: a thread counts from the moment it gets a connection until it ends, so the reply write, the draining and the follow-up count too. When either limit is reached and nothing can be closed to make room, the new connection gets 503 `busy` and nothing is stored.
 * Idle connections: a client that opens connections and sends nothing holds a place until the header deadline (10 s), then gets 408 and is closed (`test_idle_sockets_closed_at_deadline`). While they hold the 256 places, a valid request gets 503 `busy` (that is the backpressure; send it again).
